    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d751">

                <pb facs="0716" n="704" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg704"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.857/68: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Venezuela</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Corrigan</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-03-01T17:00:00-04:30">March 1, 1940—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 7:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>24. Your 14, February 29, 4 p.m. Dr. Gil Borges<anchor n="32" xml:id="d751-f1"/>
                    feels that the American position may be weakened if a protest is made before
                    statutes for the government of the continental security zone have been drawn up
                    and accepted by the 21 republics. He doubts that by this criterion the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Wakama</hi> incident would constitute a violation.
                    However, the Foreign Minister will not advise his Government to hold back from a
                    collective action provided all have agreed, and he has promised the Embassy a
                    written statement of its position after conferring with the President.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Corrigan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d751-f1" n="32" xml:id="d751-f1-content">
                <p>Esteban Gil Borges, Venezuelan Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
